          {
            "claim": "The curve is nonsingular and R is nonzero.",
            "reasoning": "The roots -ab, -ac, -bc are distinct because a,b,c are nonzero and distinct. Thus the cubic defines a nonsingular curve. A point with Y=0 is a point of order 2, so the order-3 assumption implies R is nonzero."
          },
          {
            "claim": "The order-3 condition implies T=0.",
            "reasoning": "Expanding the cubic gives X^3+VX^2+UzX+z^2, with value R^2 at X=1. Its tangent slope at S is (3+2V+Uz)/(2R). The duplication formula gives x(2S)=lambda^2-V-2; since 2S=-S, this x-coordinate is 1. Substitution and expansion yield -T=0."
          },
          {
            "claim": "1+z^2 is a rational square.",
            "reasoning": "The stated identity and T=0 give (2z^2+Uz-1)^2=4(1+z^2)R^2. Since R is a nonzero rational, division by (2R)^2 expresses 1+z^2 as a rational square."
          },
          {
            "claim": "If two entries are integers, then z is an integer.",
            "reasoning": "With A=a+b and B=ab, the substitutions U=A+z/B and V=B+Az/B in T=0 yield the displayed monic quartic with integer coefficients. Since z is rational, the rational root theorem forces z to be an integer; it is nonzero because a,b,c are nonzero."
          },
          {
            "claim": "The integer conclusion contradicts 1+z^2 being a square.",
            "reasoning": "For nonzero integer z, |z|^2<z^2+1<(|z|+1)^2, so z^2+1 cannot be an integer square. A rational square that is an integer is an integer square, giving the contradiction."
          },
          {
            "claim": "The conclusion has the stated narrow scope.",
            "reasoning": "The argument rules out two integer entries only in the specified triple when its specified point S has order exactly 3; it does not assert an obstruction for appended coordinates or for the main sextuple problem."
          }
